   "better_value": "frame",
   "reason": "The field question permits \"other\" only when \"a component is named but fits no class\". That precondition fails here: the document names the frame as a cracked component, and \"frame\" is in the closed vocabulary. Evidence at data/raw/ads/2016-05249.txt: the Unsafe Condition paragraph reports \"cracks in the frame, shear web, and shear ties adjacent to tension ties\" (:348-350; \"adjacent to tension ties\" is a location modifier, the cracks are in the frame); the purpose statement names frames as one of three cracking members (:356-359, repeated :51-53); an entire required action is headed \"(n) Retained One-Time Inspection for Cracks in Frames at Previous Tension Tie Locations\" (:549-550); and paragraph (p) requires \"post-modification detailed inspections for cracking of the tension tie and frame structure\" (:590-592). Stage 1 and Stage 2 inspections cover \"the tension ties, shear webs, and frames at body stations (STA) 1120 through 1220\" (:371, :470). Subject is ATA Code 53, Fuselage (:344). I tried to break this three ways. (1) Is the frame only an incidental neighbour, as Reader A argues? No: paragraph (n) is a standalone required inspection for cracks in frames, so the frame is a damage site in its own right. (2) Does the AD's leading member, the tension tie, have a better vocabulary slot that would make \"frame\" a distortion? No: the words floor, beam, fitting, stringer, longeron, bulkhead and skin never appear as structure (the sole \"Floor\" hit at :88 is the Docket Office street address), so floor_beam or fitting would import a taxonomy the source does not state, and the absence of a tension-tie slot does not by itself unlock \"other\" when another named damaged component does fit. (3) Is the extractor internally consistent? No: the sentence it attached as justification is the same sentence that says \"cracks in the frame\", so its own quote supports the in-vocabulary value it declined to use. Recording \"other\" discards a fact the vocabulary can express and yields a null-information value. The only reading under which \"other\" survives is if the task silently required the single most prominent component and treated a vocabulary miss as fatal; the question's explicit definition of \"other\" forecloses that, so I judge the vocabulary match decisive."
  },
